(1912785, 188367, 42995, '<td valign="top">so i''ve read the comments of others and looked at this for a long time and i think the presence of the zebra makes the shot.  in my mind''s eye, i see the photographer taking the time to carefully compose the zebra in the shot when this ostrich pops his head up just as he/she presses the shutter. <br/> <br/>i think it''s hysterical!</td>', 'so i''ve read the comments of others and looked at this for a long time and i think the presence of the zebra makes the shot.  in my mind''s eye, i see the photographer taking the time to carefully compose the zebra in the shot when this ostrich pops his head up just as he/she presses the shutter.  i think it''s hysterical!', '2005-12-01 13:42:57.000000', NULL, 0),
(1922850, 186973, 49096, '<td valign="top">awesome goose!</td>', 'awesome goose!', '2005-12-05 04:08:16.000000', NULL, 0),
(1973740, 187399, 43115, '<td valign="top">Hi Alan, thanks for your comment on my shot.<br/> <br/>I looked through your portfolio and was immediatley drawn to this shot, just from the thumbnail.  That''s always a good sign, right?  :)<br/> <br/>This is a really emotive picture.  It immediatley draws you in, and it tells a story, which is what I think all photographers strive to do.  You really feel sorry for this little guy, and you want to know what happened to fell him.  I''m quite suprised that it didn''t score better, based on how  loudly it speaks to the viewer.  I wonder if it wasn''t just too powerful for some... but you can''t let that stop you from taking the shot.<br/> <br/>Technically there''s a lot to love also.  I don''t think you did a selective desat or anything, but the contrast of the color of the bird against the grey of the asphalt is just great and really helps add to the emotion.<br/> <br/>My only suggestion is that the depth of field seems even a little too shallow.  You don''t want <i>much]</i> more, but <i>a little</i> more to bring the stones just behind him into focus too would have been nice, in my opinion.<br/> <br/>This is a really powerful shot, and you should be proud of it.</td>', 'Hi Alan, thanks for your comment on my shot. I looked through your portfolio and was immediatley drawn to this shot, just from the thumbnail.  That''s always a good sign, right?  :) This is a really emotive picture.  It immediatley draws you in, and it tells a story, which is what I think all photographers strive to do.  You really feel sorry for this little guy, and you want to know what happened to fell him.  I''m quite suprised that it didn''t score better, based on how  loudly it speaks to the viewer.  I wonder if it wasn''t just too powerful for some... but you can''t let that stop you from taking the shot. Technically there''s a lot to love also.  I don''t think you did a selective desat or anything, but the contrast of the color of the bird against the grey of the asphalt is just great and really helps add to the emotion. My only suggestion is that the depth of field seems even a little too shallow.  You don''t want much] more, but a little more to bring the stones just behind him into focus too would have been nice, in my opinion. This is a really powerful shot, and you should be proud of it.', '2005-12-23 11:29:52.000000', NULL, 0),
